        "text": "Neural Processes (NPs) (Garnelo et al., 2018b;a) are a class of methods that approximate the probabilistic distribution of the continuous function conditioned on partial observations. Suppose we have a labeled context set DC = (XC , YC) := (xi,yi)i\u2208C sampled from a continuous function with inputs xi and outputs yi, NPs target at predicting an arbitrary and finite number of target points DT = (XT , YT ) := (xi,yi)i\u2208T by learning the input-output mapping function f . Given some observations (a set of context points) of a signal, many possible functions may match well to these observations and thus naturally there exists uncertainty on the functions. The conditional distributions of targets points can be modeled as:\np\u03d5(YT |XT , Dc) = \u220f\n(x,y)\u2208DT N (y;\u00b5y(x, Dc), \u03c32y(x, Dc)). (1)\nTo generate coherent function predictions and better model the function distributions, Garnelo et al. (Garnelo et al., 2018b) introduce the (Latent) Neural Process by encoding the global structure and uncertainty of the function into a latent Gaussian variable via Bayesian inference:\nz \u223c p\u03b8(z|XT , DC); p\u03b8(YT |XT , z) = \u220f\n(x,y)\u2208DT N (y;\u00b5y(z, XT , DC), \u03c32y(z, XT , DC)). (2)\nDue to the intractable log-likelihood, some previous works adopt amortized variational inference (Kingma & Welling, 2014), which is also used to optimize our proposed framework. We can derive the evidence lower bound (ELBO) on log p\u03b8(YT |XT , z), where the ELBO can be viewed as a combination of the reconstruction term (the first term) and the KL term (the second term):\nEz\u223cq\u03d5(z|DT )[log p\u03b8(YT |z, XT , DC)]\u2212DKL[q\u03d5(z|DT )||p\u03c8(z|XT , DC)]. (3)\nHere, \u03c8, \u03d5 and \u03b8 refer to the parameters of conditional prior encoder, conditional posterior encoder and the decoder, similar to conditional VAEs (Sohn et al., 2015; Ivanov et al., 2019).\nSo far, we have revisited the problem formulation of NPs. Since NPs approximate the distribution over functions with some function observations, they can also be regarded as probabilistic, continuous, conditional generative models, where the generation result is a continuous function."
